-- Passage rg7-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('97bbbdaf-e60f-5c37-a446-17680f7cbadf', 'rg7-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Bridge Debate of 1887$q$, $q$In 1887, the river town of Calder Falls faced a hard decision. The only way across the wide Tamsen River was a slow ferry that carried six wagons at a time. A group of shop owners proposed building an iron bridge, and the town held a public meeting to debate the idea.

Supporters of the bridge made three main points. They showed records from the ferry office proving that farmers waited an average of two hours to cross during harvest season. They pointed out that the town of Merrow, forty miles north, had built a bridge in 1880, and its number of shops had doubled since then. They also argued that a bridge would let doctors reach families on the far side quickly in bad weather, when the ferry often stopped running.

Opponents raised two concerns. The first was cost. The bridge would cost about 18,000 dollars, which was more than the town spent in three full years. The second concern came from Elias Brandt, the owner of the ferry. He claimed that iron bridges were dangerous and that "everyone knows" they fall down. He offered no examples.

After two evenings of debate, the town voted to build the bridge. To handle the cost, the town agreed to pay for it slowly with a small toll on each wagon. The bridge opened in 1890. Town records show that the tolls covered the full cost within twelve years.$q$, 'draft')
