-- Passage rg7-argument-evidence-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8124ef14-7e90-54c4-9256-ea3fddb82c7c', 'rg7-argument-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Lower the Hoops$q$, $q$In most youth basketball leagues, eight-year-olds shoot at the same ten-foot hoops that professional players use. For a child who is four feet tall, that is like an adult shooting at a basket fifteen feet high. Youth leagues should lower the hoops to eight feet for players under ten.

Lower hoops would help young players learn good shooting form. When a basket is too high, small children must heave the ball with both arms from their chest instead of shooting with one hand. Coaches say these habits are hard to break later.

Lower hoops would also make the game more fun. A research team at a state university ran a study with 120 young players. Half played on eight-foot hoops and half on ten-foot hoops. The players on lower hoops made more than twice as many baskets and passed the ball more often. In interviews afterward, most of them said they enjoyed the games more.

Finally, more success leads to more practice. Children who enjoy a sport are more likely to keep playing, and the more they play, the better they get.

Some coaches argue that players should use regulation hoops from the start so they do not have to adjust later. However, young players already use smaller balls and play shorter games. Equipment that fits the player is normal in almost every youth sport.

Basketball is a game of skill, not just height. Lowering the hoops would give young players a fair chance to build that skill.$q$, 'draft')
